  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two growth outcomes at 475 °C for silicon-containing precursor chemistry. On the left, using only silane (SiH4) appears to produce a compact cluster of blue spheres on a flat substrate, representing a silicon deposit or nanoparticle assembly. Arrows pointing upward and sideways suggest continued growth and spreading from this aggregate.\n\nOn the right, adding ethylene (C2H4) together with SiH4 changes the result dramatically. Instead of a simple stacked silicon cluster, the surface is covered by a linked silicon–carbon network. Blue spheres still mark larger silicon nodes, but they are now embedded in a framework of Si and C atoms connected by bonds above the substrate. The drawing suggests carbon incorporation stabilizes or bridges silicon sites, creating an extended hybrid Si–C structure rather than uncontrolled silicon accumulation.\n\nOverall, the main message is that introducing a carbon-containing co-reactant alters the deposition mechanism at the same temperature: pure silane favors silicon particle-like growth, whereas silane plus ethylene leads to formation of a more interconnected silicon–carbon material on the surface.",
